.TH ORACLE 8 "2026-04-27" "CECS 378" "CECS 378 Buffer Overflow Lab"
.SH NAME
oracle \- the user account that owns the flags; speaks only through setuid binaries
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B oracle
is a system user on
.BR mainframe (7).

.B oracle
owns three files:
.IR /srv/flag1.txt ,
.IR /srv/flag2.txt ,
and
.IR /srv/honor.txt .
All three are mode 0400, readable only by their owner.

.B oracle
does not log in. Its login shell is
.BR nologin (8).
You cannot
.BR su (1)
into
.BR oracle .
You cannot
.BR sudo (8)
as
.BR oracle .

You can, however, ask
.B oracle
to read on your behalf, because three programs on this system run with
.B oracle's
effective user id when you execute them: they are setuid. The
.BR chmod (1)
mode bit
.B 4755
is what makes a binary "speak in
.B oracle's
voice."

The setuid binaries are:
.TP
.BR vuln1 (1),
.BR vuln2 (1)
The targets. These were not designed to take dictation. Convince them
to take dictation anyway.
.TP
.BR pledge (1)
The honor-pledge utility. This one was designed to read on your
behalf, but only after you affirm authorship of your exploit.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /srv/flag1.txt
Phase Phi flag.
.TP
.I /srv/flag2.txt
Phase Omega flag.
.TP
.I /srv/honor.txt
Honor flag (released by
.BR pledge (1)).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mainframe (7),
.BR vuln1 (1),
.BR vuln2 (1),
.BR pledge (1),
.BR chmod (1),
.BR setuid (2)
.SH BUGS
.B oracle
is not the bug.
.B oracle
follows orders. The bug is that
.BR vuln1 (1)
and
.BR vuln2 (1)
take orders from anyone with a long enough string.
